## Scope and preflight

Run only on an explicit `/save-session-maintenance` request. It authorizes maintenance
of `__PROJECT__`'s current status note, its active log and directly affected archive-chain
links, not a corpus-wide cleanup or recovery of other transcripts. A bloat
reminder or standing recording grant is not an invocation. Do not grant/revoke recording
consent or advance `.claude/.last-saved-session` during maintenance.

Resolve the checkout's physical root (`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`, then `pwd -P`;
use `pwd -P` outside Git). Verify this installed maintenance skill's current rendered
version/project and the resolved project identity with read-only `check-drift.sh check`.
Stop on drift/mismatch or missing/incompatible prerequisites: installed CLI major 1 and
connected BM 1.x `bm_read`, `bm_search`, `bm_project`, `bm_write`, `bm_edit`, `bm_move`.
Consult installed `bm_man` if uncertain; do not install/upgrade automatically. Standing
recording consent is unnecessary for this explicitly requested maintenance; host tool
approvals still apply.

Pass `project="__PROJECT__"` on every scoped call. Use `output_format="json"` for
structured reads/receipts. Immediately before every mutation of an existing note, fresh
`bm_read` it and pass its checksum as `expected_checksum` to `bm_edit(command=...)`,
`bm_move` or an explicitly required `bm_write(overwrite=true)`. Create confirmed-missing
notes with `bm_write(overwrite=false)`, retaining returned identifiers.

Verify each receipt against the installed contract:

| Operation | Require |
|---|---|
| `bm_write` / `bm_edit` | a documented successful `action`, the intended returned path/identifier, and a nonempty `checksum` |
| note `bm_move` | `action="moved"`, `previous_file_path`, and the actual destination `file_path` (also check `requested_file_path` if returned); `checksum` may be null — fresh-read the destination for a usable one before further mutations; never apply the distinct directory-move receipt schema to a note move |

A missing project field is not failure if the installed contract omits it. Refuse
explicitly conflicting qualified scope. On `isError`, typed refusal, `REVISION_CONFLICT`,
malformed receipt or uncertain outcome, stop and report confirmed partial work. Never
blindly repeat an append/move or overwrite. Do not use filesystem writes to bypass MCP.

## Step 0 — Run a memory-doctor health check

Run `bm doctor --skip-self-test --json --project __PROJECT__` before Step 1. It is
read-only. Record each finding's `check`, `severity`, `summary` and any suggested
`command`. Never run a suggested command: fixes need the user's separate, later
agreement.

## Step 1 — Resolve and roll over the active log

Read `status/__PROJECT__-current-status.md` using `bm_read`. Resolve the last wikilink
in the most recent dated Status Summary entry to the active session log. Only if there
is no link, fall back to scoped `bm_search(query="__PROJECT__ session")`; inspect credible
results and ask the user to choose if ambiguous. Do not select a log by search ranking
or mtime alone. If none exists, report no log to maintain; this skill does not bootstrap
a routine save.

Read the resolved note. At 300 lines or more:
1. Find its first dated entry (`## YYYY-MM-DD`). Create a note titled
   `Session Log — <earliest-date>` under `sessions/`, copying the full body and preserving
   its frontmatter style. Add a banner naming the date range, the prior archived log
   (if any) and the active note's continuation. Verify the creation receipt and retain
   its actual identifier. A collision is a stop, not permission to overwrite.
2. If the directly preceding archived note links forward to the active note, fresh-read
   and target-edit that link to the newly created archive, using its checksum.
3. Fresh-read the active log and replace its body with a short “Continues from
   [[<new archive>]]” banner using checksum-guarded `bm_write(overwrite=true)`; preserve
   its title/permalink and frontmatter. Verify the receipt. This explicit rollover is
   the bounded exception to routine saves' append-only policy.

Under 300 lines, leave it unchanged. No unrelated archive chain traversal or cleanup.

## Step 2 — Triage the current status note

### Capture Open-item staleness before triage

Before any triage/condensing below, run
`${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/skills/setup-memory-workflow/scripts/check-drift.sh
open-staleness` and capture its full output verbatim — one `<path>\t<sessions-untouched-count>`
line per stale `open-items/` note — for the rollup subsection later in this step; nothing/exit
0 means no rollup work. Capturing it now, before triage, matters: `stale_open_items()` counts
each dated Status Summary entry as one session, and the triage below may condense several
dated entries into a single range entry (e.g. `**2026-11-07 → 2026-11-16:**`) — a condensed
range entry counts as only one session, dated by its start date, so capturing the stale list
after condensing would undercount sessions and most items would never reach
`STALE_SESSION_THRESHOLD`. (A longer-term alternative — counting session-log boundaries
instead of Status Summary dated entries — is noted in the design doc as a future option, not
required now.) Do not re-run `open-staleness` again later in this step; reuse this captured
list for the rollup below.

Assess the status note independently for: a size/token-limit read failure, roughly 8,000
characters or 200 lines, or a single entry over roughly 800 characters. No signals means
no triage. A limited response is not the whole note: use documented bounded `bm_read`
sections/line ranges and continuation information. If full content or a fresh checksum
cannot be obtained safely, stop and report the limitation; do not edit from a truncated
view. A host-provided full-response temporary file may be inspected read-only.

Extract stable reference facts or still-real known issues to a relevant existing/new
note under `reference/` or `known-issues/`, leaving a concise `See [[Note Title]]` pointer.
Preserve Open items and task-tracker authority (now including items rolled into the Stale
Open Items Index — that index is also authoritative for stale items, not just the live
Open section). Prefer archival to deletion; this skill
does not authorize outright deletion. Use `bm_move(identifier=..., destination="archive/",
expected_checksum=...)` for whole-note archival where appropriate, then verify the
returned identity, and repair and re-verify directly affected links using returned
paths; a move does not guarantee links survive. Avoid changing the current status/log
identity.

If using installed `memory-defrag` or `memory-lifecycle` guidance, restrict it to these
notes and this scope; adapt retired calls to BM 1.x, fresh checksums and verified receipts.
Their broader defaults do not expand this request. Reassess after bounded targeted edits;
if further work would expand scope or lose uncertain material, report it and ask rather
than repeatedly rewriting until thresholds are met.

### Stale Open Items Index rollup

Using the stale list captured above (not a fresh `open-staleness` run), for each returned
`<path>`:
1. Fresh-read that `open-items/` note; set its `status` from `open` to `stale` via
   checksum-guarded `bm_edit`. This step is not optional: `check-drift.sh open-staleness`
   only ever considers notes with `status: open`, so skipping this transition would make
   the same already-indexed item get reported as newly stale again on every future
   maintenance run.
2. Replace that item's individual `- [<category>] <short title> — [[Open: <slug>]]`
   pointer line in the status note's Open section with (or fold its count into) a single
   rollup line: `5+ sessions untouched: <count> items — [[Stale Open Items Index —
   __PROJECT__]]`, where `<count>` is the total number of items now folded into that
   rollup line. (The `5` mirrors `STALE_SESSION_THRESHOLD` in `lib-open-items.sh`; keep
   this literal in sync if that constant is ever retuned. This bullet itself counts toward
   `OPEN_LINE_CAP` like any other Open bullet — that's expected, not a bug to fix.)
3. Add or update that item's grouped pointer line — same pointer shape as a live Open
   entry, `- [<category>] <title> — [[Open: <slug>]]`, so the wikilink resolves against
   the same `Open: <slug>` note title used when the item was created or converted —
   preserving its original `[category]`, in the `Stale Open Items Index — __PROJECT__`
   note: `bm_write(overwrite=false)` to create the index note on first use, checksum-guarded
   `bm_edit` append thereafter. Group lines by the item's original `[category]`.

Verify every receipt per the contract table above before moving to the next item. Then
run `bm_schema(command="validate", note_type="open-item", project="__PROJECT__")` — this
is read-only, same as Step 0's `memory-doctor` check — and fold any findings into the
completion summary; never auto-fix them.

### On-request legacy Open-item conversion

Convert a legacy (pre-pointer, freeform-prose) Open item to the pointer shape only when
the user names one or more specific items for conversion in this maintenance request.
Never a bulk/automatic sweep of every legacy item still in prose form — the same
explicit-invocation, no-sweep discipline this template already applies to its
reference-fact triage above. Absent that explicit naming, leave legacy prose bullets
untouched, exactly as the save-session template's forward-only rule already does.

For each named item: find its prose bullet in the status note's Open section and infer
`found_session` from the dated Status Summary entry it originally appeared under (walk
back through the log/archive chain if it predates the current status note); infer
`severity` from any existing "(low priority)"-style marker in its text, omitting the
field if none is present. On confirmed-missing `open-items/` directory or schema note,
apply the same lazy schema provisioning as save-session's creation path (create the
`type: schema`/`entity: open-item` note first; consult `bm_man(page="bm-schema(3)")` if
uncertain). Create its `open-items/<slug>.md` note with title `Open: <slug>` (not the
filename alone — the pointer line is a Basic Memory wikilink, which resolves by note
title) and directory `open-items`, carrying frontmatter `type: open-item`, `status: open`,
the inferred `found_session`, `last_touched_session` set to today, and
the item's `[category]` text as its body, using `bm_write(overwrite=false)`. Then
fresh-read the status note and replace that one prose bullet — and only that bullet —
with the standard pointer line from the save-session template:
`- [<category>] <short title> — [[Open: <slug>]]`, via checksum-guarded `bm_edit`. Verify
both receipts per the contract table above before reporting the conversion done; a
partial pair (note created but pointer not swapped, or vice versa) is a stop, not a
best-effort finish.

## On completion or failure

Confirm touched note titles and returned permalinks/paths, final status size if triaged,
and any no-op. Include Step 0's findings or its clean result, listing any suggested
commands without running them. On failure identify the step, error and confirmed partial
changes. Preserve all unknown outcomes for inspection before retry.
